' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' mustache/escape.mfb — the HTML escaping a `{{name}}` tag applies.
'
' Not `encoding::htmlEscape`, and the difference is deliberate. That function
' escapes the five XML metacharacters and writes an apostrophe as `&apos;`,
' which is correct XML and correct HTML5 but is NOT what a Mustache template
' produces: every reference implementation writes `&#39;`, and `&apos;` is not a
' character reference HTML 4 or an XHTML-as-text/html document defines. A
' template rendered here has to be byte-comparable with one rendered anywhere
' else, so the escaping is the reference implementation's, character for
' character, and that means three characters `encoding::htmlEscape` leaves
' alone:
'
'   /  as `&#x2F;`   closes a `<script>` block early inside a string literal
'   `  as `&#x60;`   is an attribute delimiter in older Internet Explorer
'   =  as `&#x3D;`   starts an attribute in an unquoted attribute value
'
' Each of those turns an unquoted or single-quoted attribute into an injection
' point, which is why mustache.js, Handlebars and their descendants all escape
' them. Escaping more than the minimum is always safe -- an over-escaped
' document renders identically -- so this is strictly the stronger of the two.
'
' The ampersand is replaced FIRST so that the `&` each replacement introduces is
' not escaped a second time; none of the other seven replacements produces a
' character any later replacement matches.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORT strings

' Escape the eight characters a Mustache `{{name}}` tag escapes. Exported from
' the package as `mustache::escapeHtml`; named differently here so the public
' wrapper in lib.mfb can carry the DOC block without shadowing this one.
PUBLIC FUNC escapeText(text AS String) AS String
  MUT out AS String = strings::replace(text, "&", "&amp;")
  out = strings::replace(out, "<", "&lt;")
  out = strings::replace(out, ">", "&gt;")
  out = strings::replace(out, "\"", "&quot;")
  out = strings::replace(out, "'", "&#39;")
  out = strings::replace(out, "/", "&#x2F;")
  out = strings::replace(out, "`", "&#x60;")
  RETURN strings::replace(out, "=", "&#x3D;")
END FUNC
